title: 'identity model: machine key as trust anchor + OPTIONAL per-agent attestation via machine-signed in-band cert (mechanism b); preserve one-connection-per-machine'
---
DECISION (Max, 2026-07-07, grilling Q2): keep the machine Ed25519 identity as the relay trust anchor and add an OPTIONAL per-agent attestation layer. Preserve one supervised machine-wide relay connector service/control plane; do not require a connector process or relay control-plane registration per agent. This does not promise exactly one network socket internally.

The machine identity key may sign a short certificate binding agent key -> machine; the certificate rides in an additive application-envelope version; the recipient verifies the machine certificate and then the agent signature. This must not change relay registration topology or require relay-side agent-key registration. Messages without an agent attestation remain valid for backward compatibility.

B200 has since shipped the operational half of this model: one HOME-scoped supervised relay connector dynamically serves all repository brokers while aliases retain distinct relay registrations. B207 adds advisory provenance classes (`same_repo`, `same_host`, `relay`, `unknown`), but explicitly provides neither cryptographic identity nor authority. Neither change implements the optional per-agent certificate proposed here.

Rationale: co-located agents can impersonate each other at the OS level, so attesting the machine is the honest base claim; a per-agent signature is an opt-in distinction. Rejected alternatives remain TOFU, which defers spoof risk to first contact, and relay-registered agent keys, which break the machine-wide connector/control-plane design.

Implementation remains deferred under trusted-swarm-first. Before implementation, write an ADR covering certificate canonicalization, expiry/rotation/revocation, replay resistance, rename/resume behavior, schema-v2 fields, encrypted-envelope interaction, key lifecycle, and whether identity follows alias, session, or durable agent instance. Do not precommit to an OpenSSH `.ed25519.ssh` artifact: either reuse the existing raw per-alias key with explicit semantics or define a distinct session-attestation key path. I002 v1 intentionally excludes identity/trust fields.

A valid machine or agent certificate proves key binding only. It must not upgrade a relay message to `same_host`/`same_repo`, establish a common operator, approve an action, or bypass the B098 bus-never-RPC boundary.

Related: I003 cryptographic trust tiers depends on this; B097 unified listing surfaces identity scope; B098 preserves the approval-path lockdown invariant.
